---
import DocsLayout from "../../../components/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const manifest = `{
  "manifest_version": 1,
  "id": "dev.example.worklog",
  "name": "Worklog (development)",
  "homepage": "http://localhost:5173/",
  "redirect_uris": ["http://localhost:5173/"],
  "requirements": {
    "access": "full_collection",
    "contracts": [],
    "capabilities": {
      "contract_version": 2,
      "required": ["collection.read", "records.create", "records.edit"]
    }
  }
}`;

const entry = `import { createMdbase } from "@mdbase-dev/sdk";
import manifest from "./mdbase-app.json"; // the development or the deployed one

export const mdbase = createMdbase({
  id: manifest.id,
  name: manifest.name,
  origin: new URL(manifest.homepage).origin, // "http://localhost:5173"
  environment: "production",
  manifest,
});`;

const validate = `npx mdbase-connect-dev validate-manifest mdbase-app.dev.json --allow-local
npx mdbase-connect-dev validate-manifest mdbase-app.json`;

const port = `import { defineConfig } from "vite";

export default defineConfig({
  server: { port: 5173, strictPort: true },
});`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Develop on localhost"
  description="Run your app from a local development server against real mdbase accounts and collections."
  active="local-development"
  headings={[
    { href: "#rule", label: "What is accepted" },
    { href: "#manifest", label: "A development manifest" },
    { href: "#entry", label: "Declare the app" },
    { href: "#separate", label: "A separate application" },
    { href: "#consent", label: "What the user sees" },
    { href: "#redirect", label: "Redirect URIs" },
    { href: "#server", label: "Development servers" },
    { href: "#no-account", label: "Working with no account" },
    { href: "#problems", label: "Common problems" }
  ]}
>
  <p>
    You develop against the same mdbase service your users use. There is no
    development mode, sandbox key or allowlist to apply for.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>SDK version</strong>
    <p>
      Use an <code>@mdbase-dev/sdk</code> release after
      {" "}<code>0.1.0-beta.168</code>. Earlier releases refuse an
      {" "}<code>http://localhost</code> origin and fail to load under
      {" "}<code>vite dev</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="rule">What is accepted</h2>
  <p>
    Your app’s origin may be plain HTTP when it is on this computer:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>http://localhost:&lt;port&gt;</code></li>
    <li><code>http://127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;</code></li>
    <li><code>http://[::1]:&lt;port&gt;</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Any port works. Every other app origin must be HTTPS, including a
    development machine reached by its LAN address or a tunnel hostname.
  </p>

  <h2 id="manifest">A development manifest</h2>
  <p>
    A manifest names the origin its app is served from, so keep two: the one
    you publish, with your HTTPS homepage, and one for development whose
    {" "}<code>homepage</code> and <code>redirect_uris</code> use the
    development server’s origin.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={manifest} language="json" label="mdbase-app.dev.json" />
  <p>
    Everything else can be the same. You can keep two files, or write both
    from one script as the <a href="/sdk/connect-quickstart/#manifest">Worklog tutorial</a>
    {" "}does.
  </p>
  <p>
    The manifest checker refuses an HTTP homepage by default, so that a
    development manifest is caught before it ships. Pass
    {" "}<code>--allow-local</code> for the development one:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={validate} language="sh" label="Terminal" />
  <p>
    The published
    {" "}<a href="/connect/schemas/mdbase-app.v1.json">JSON Schema</a>
    {" "}describes the deployed form, so an editor that validates against it
    marks an HTTP homepage. The checker with <code>--allow-local</code> is the
    authority for a development manifest.
  </p>

  <h2 id="entry">Declare the app</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={entry} language="ts" label="mdbase.ts" />
  <p>
    <code>environment</code> stays <code>"production"</code>. It names the
    mdbase service your app talks to, and that is the same one in development.
    Only <code>origin</code> and the manifest change between development and
    your deployed site.
  </p>

  <h2 id="separate">A separate application</h2>
  <p>
    mdbase identifies an app by its manifest and binds what a user approves to
    the exact origin, port included. Your localhost build is therefore its own
    application:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Collections approved on <code>localhost:5173</code> are approved for
      that origin only. Your deployed app asks its users separately.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sign-in state and the on-device copy of a collection belong to the
      origin. Changing the port starts again from signed out.
    </li>
    <li>
      Give the development manifest its own <code>name</code>, such as
      “Worklog (development)”, so the two are easy to tell apart on your
      account page.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The records are real. A development build that opens your own collection
    reads and writes your own files, so use a collection made for testing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="consent">What the user sees</h2>
  <p>
    When a localhost app asks for access, the approval screen calls it “an
    app running on this computer”, says its identity is unverified, and shows
    no icon. A page on the internet cannot give itself a localhost origin, so
    the only program that can be there is one already running on the person’s
    machine. The screen says that plainly instead of presenting the manifest’s
    name as a brand.
  </p>
  <p>
    Account sign-in, the collection choice and the list of capabilities are
    the same as for a deployed app.
  </p>

  <h2 id="redirect">Redirect URIs</h2>
  <p>
    <code>redirect_uris</code> must list at least one URL on the app’s origin.
    A browser app needs no page there. Sign-in happens in a window that the
    SDK opens, and the SDK learns the result itself; that window never
    navigates to your redirect URI and no token is delivered to it. Listing
    the homepage is enough.
  </p>
  <p>
    The redirect URI is used when you pass your own sign-in opener, as a
    native shell or browser extension does. mdbase then sends the person’s
    browser to <code>returnUrl</code> when sign-in ends, to bring them back to
    your app. It carries no credential, so any page or deep link of yours can
    receive it. See <a href="/sdk/lifecycle/#opener">Sign-in outside a popup</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="server">Development servers</h2>
  <p>
    The SDK runs its engine in a Web Worker and loads WebAssembly files that
    ship inside the package. With Vite, <code>vite dev</code> and
    {" "}<code>vite build</code> both work with the default configuration.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fix the port, since the manifest names it:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={port} language="ts" label="vite.config.ts" />
  <p>
    With another bundler, the requirement is that the files under the
    package’s <code>dist/</code> are served from your own origin, beside the
    SDK’s modules. For a page with no bundler, use the
    {" "}<a href="/sdk/portable-apps/">browser bundle</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="no-account">Working with no account</h2>
  <p>
    Much of an app can be built before you sign in at all:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>createMdbase(declaration, &#123; unsynced: true &#125;)</code>
      {" "}opens a real collection kept in the browser. See
      {" "}<a href="/sdk/unsynced/">Work before connecting</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>MemoryReplica</code> and <code>createApplicationFixture()</code>
      {" "}run the same client in tests. See <a href="/sdk/testing/">Testing</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="problems">Common problems</h2>
  <table class="api-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>What you see</th>
        <th>Cause</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>“The app couldn’t load” as soon as the page opens</td>
        <td>
          The SDK release predates localhost support, or the SDK’s files are
          not served from your origin.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The entry refuses to start with an origin error</td>
        <td>
          <code>origin</code> differs from the page’s origin, or from the
          manifest’s <code>homepage</code> origin. <code>localhost</code> and
          {" "}<code>127.0.0.1</code> are different origins.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Signed out after restarting the development server</td>
        <td>The server picked another port. Set <code>strictPort</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The checker reports <code>/homepage</code></td>
        <td>
          An HTTP homepage needs <code>--allow-local</code>, or
          {" "}<code>allowLocal: true</code> when you call
          {" "}<code>validateAppManifest()</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The sign-in window does not open</td>
        <td>
          <code>signIn()</code> was called after an <code>await</code>. Call
          it directly in the click handler.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</DocsLayout>
